From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Desidero <desidero@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 18:06:51 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] perf(web): prefetch the next older history page while a
 scroll-up seeks

Older history only goes on the wire once the loader decides to land a
page, and on touch that decision waits for the fling to settle, so the
reader stops at the top and waits a full round trip for every page. Even
with 100-item pages, a slow link still leaves the reader parked at the top
for part of the way back.

The history loader now fetches the next older page as soon as a seeking
gesture brings the pane within the load threshold, ahead of the landing
decision; `loadMoreHistory` then lands the prefetched page from memory.
One page per cursor is ever in flight, a failed prefetch is forgotten so
the reader's own request retries it, and the page is dropped with its
conversation. A downward gesture, a new history window or the pane's own
movement never prefetches.

The per-gesture chaining budget is now counted in items (600, the bound
the 30 x 20-item budget gave) rather than pages, so a larger scroll-up
page does not multiply how much one gesture can page in with the
reader's hands off: with 100-item pages, 30 pages would be 3,000 items.

The larger scroll-up page this patch used to carry is upstream now (100
items for every viewport), so only the prefetch and the item budget remain.

Not upstream yet: proposed from fix/history-prefetch; drop this copy once it merges.

Docs: none (performance: older history loads ahead of the scroll; no setting)
